WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.009
YUFENG GUO: Welcome
to "AI Adventures,"

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.000
where we explore
the art, science,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.190
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:08.710
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.060
we'll train a scikit-learn
model and deploy it

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.640
on Google Cloud ML Engine.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.420
Take this information and use
it to set up your own deployment

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.360
pipeline and you'll
never need to worry

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.630
about how to deploy and
scale your models again.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.820
Say you have some
data, you've trained up

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.460
on a scikit-learn
model, and now you

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.049
want to set up a
prediction server.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.180
Let's see how you'd do
that based on the code

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.530
that we had in a previous
episode about zoo animals.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.300
First, we got to
export the model,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.180
so we'll use the joblib
library from sklearn.externals.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.680
We can use joblib.dump to
export the model to a file.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.840
Once we commit and
run this, we'll

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.500
be able to retrieve the
output from our Kaggle kernel.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.900
And here we are
ready for download.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.110
With our trained
scikit-learn model in hand,

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.530
we are ready to head over to
Google Cloud Machine Learning

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.250
Engine and load up the
model to serve predictions.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.660
That's right, you can get
all the auto scaling secured

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.920
REST API goodness not only
for TensorFlow, but also

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.920
for scikit-learn.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.790
Cloud ML Engine has
traditionally worked only

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.330
for TensorFlow, but now it also
supports scikit-learn as well

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.650
as XGBoost.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.860
This enables you to easily
transition back and forth

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.370
between scikit-learn and
TensorFlow and XGBoost

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:39.820
without having to rework your
plumbing or architecture.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.150
The first step to getting
our model in the cloud

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.900
is to upload the joblib file
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.090
It is required that
the name of the file

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.600
be literally model.joblib.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.270
So be sure to stick
it inside a folder

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.280
with a name you'll
remember, otherwise you'll

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.580
end up with more models later
and they'll all be the name.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.780
So we'll use that folder name
to locate the model later.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.560
Now let's create our
model and version,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.470
and we got to make sure we
specify that we're loading up

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.970
a scikit-learn model and select
the runtime version of Cloud ML

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.490
Engine as well as
the version of Python

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.400
that we used to
export this model.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.740
Since we were running on
Kaggle, that's Python 3.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.730
And well, that's really
all there is to it.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:31.800
We now have a scikit-learn
model serving in the cloud.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.280
Now, of course, a scalable
model is no use to anyone

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.660
without being able to
call those predictions.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.270
So let's take a simple
look at how that is done.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.090
I've gone ahead and
pulled one sample row

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.600
from our data whose answer
should be category 4.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.000
Now, what we can do
is present that data

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.410
to Cloud ML Engine as just a
simple array encoded as a JSON

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:56.120
file.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.100
Here I'm taking the test
features data frame,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.890
and then we'll extract
row 10 from it,

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.760
and then we'll call .values to
get the underlying numpy array.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.680
Unfortunately,
numpy arrays don't

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.090
print with commas
between their values.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.690
So we really want the commas.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.240
So we then wrapped it and turned
the numpy array into a Python

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.830
list to be printed out.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.880
So we have our list, we have
it saved to an input file,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.930
and now we can call
the prediction REST API

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.370
for our scikit-learn model.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.960
G Cloud has a built in
utility to do that for us,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.350
and we're expecting a response
of 4, and yes, we got it.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.110
You can follow these
steps in this video

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.530
to deploy your scikit-learn
model to production,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.920
or even step it up by turning
it into an entire automated

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.310
pipeline so that, every
time you make a new model,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.830
it can get deployed
out so you can test it.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.250
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.950
And if you enjoyed it,
please hit the Like button

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.740
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.030
when they come out.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.180
For now, head on over
to Cloud ML Engine

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.280
and upload your
scikit-learn model

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.890
to get auto scaling predictions
right at your fingertips.

